Subject: [PATCH] fix(quant): accumulate block-wise FP8 matmuls in float32

The block-wise FP8 matmul kept its accumulator in BF16. Each 128-wide
input block's float32 dot rounded to BF16, the block's float32 scale
rounded to BF16, and the running sum rounded after every block.
xla_quantized_matmul_local then rounded each shard's partial sum to
BF16 before a row-parallel layer's all-reduce. A BF16 forward rounds
the product once.

On a v5p-64 at --tp-size 32 (2026-09-27) GLM-5.3's capture check
failed layers 10 to 13 at 2.16 to 2.75 times a BF16 forward's error.
Its q_a_proj and kv_a_proj_with_mqa sum 48 input blocks each.

The kernel now keeps each block's dot, its scales and the running sum
in float32, and takes an out_dtype. xla_quantized_matmul_local asks it
for float32 when a reduction follows, reduces in float32 and rounds
once.

Checked on CPU against GLM-5.3's published weights, one decoder layer
at a time, with the float32 reference's hidden states as input and
this kernel's arithmetic written out in torch (layers 8 to 13 and 20,
2026-09-27): each layer's own error sat at 1.39 to 2.27 times a BF16
forward's before, 1.06 to 1.11 with the float32 accumulator and scale,
and 1.00 to 1.08 with the float32 reduction too. The kernel itself,
run in the Pallas TPU interpreter on a 64 x 6,144 by 6,144 x 512
product, went from 3.96 to 0.71 times a BF16 matmul's error.
